        <div id="why-different" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why an Epoxy Garage Floor Needs Different Tools
          </h2>

          <p>
            An epoxy garage floor is not a kitchen floor, and the household mop in the closet is usually the wrong tool for it. The coating is non-porous and glossy, so dirt sits on top instead of soaking in, which is good news. The bad news is that the gloss lives in a thin clear topcoat, and the thing that wears it away is grit: sand, road salt crystals and the fine aggregate a car carries in on its tires. Every time a two-ton car rolls over that grit, it grinds a little gloss off. A mop that pushes grit around instead of lifting it makes that worse.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size is the second difference. A two-car garage is typically 400 to 500 square feet of open floor, often bigger than the whole kitchen and living room combined, with no rugs or furniture breaking it up. The 12 inch heads on household spin mops and sweepers turn that into a slog, and a tool that makes a job slow is a tool that gets skipped. The right garage kit is wider than what you use in the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, the floor is slick when wet. A full-gloss epoxy with no anti-slip additive is slippery under a film of water and dangerous under a film of soap. That pushes you toward tools that put down very little water, microfiber flat pads and spray mops, and toward a squeegee to get rinse water off quickly, rather than a dripping string mop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, many garage floors are textured. Vinyl flake broadcasts and anti-slip aggregates are common on professionally installed systems and in better DIY kits. Texture snags cheap microfiber, traps water that a stiff squeegee bridges over, and can shred lightweight pads. Choosing the mop for your floor means knowing which kind of surface you have. If you are not sure what is on yours, our guide to the <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">best garage floor epoxy</a> explains the common systems and topcoats.
          </p>

          <p>
            This page is about the tools. For the cleaning routine itself, how often to dust and wet mop, the annual deep clean and how to deal with individual stains, read our guide on <a href="/how-to-clean-epoxy-garage-floor/">how to clean an epoxy garage floor</a>. Here we cover what to buy so that routine actually happens.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The 5 Best Mops and Squeegees for Epoxy Floors
          </h2>

          <p>
            We chose these on what can be checked: head or blade width, material, what is in the box, pad laundering ratings and care instructions from the maker, and how well each design suits a large, slick, sometimes textured coating. We did not run our own floor tests. Specifications come from manufacturer and retailer listings, and where listings disagree, on handle length or bottle capacity for example, we say so rather than pick a number.
          </p>

