        <div id="dust-mop" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Dust Mop Does Most of the Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coating installers commonly describe regular dry removal of grit as the most important maintenance step, because abrasion is what wears topcoats. That makes the dust mop the most important tool in the kit, ahead of anything wet. A good one lifts and holds fine grit rather than flicking it, and it is wide enough that a full pass of the garage takes minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Width is simple arithmetic. A 36 inch head with a few inches of overlap covers about two and a half feet per pass. A 22 foot wide two-car garage is therefore about eight or nine passes front to back. A 12 inch household sweeper covers under a foot per pass and needs more than 20. People will happily do the first every week and will rarely do the second. If your garage is single-car or crowded with storage, a 24 inch head is easier to steer between obstacles; for open two- and three-car floors, 36 inches is the sweet spot, and 48 inches starts to be awkward to turn and store.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cotton and microfiber both work. Cotton dust mops, like the O-Cedar kit at the top of our list, are traditional, cheap and durable, and they hold grit well in their loops or cut ends. Microfiber dust pads grab finer dust and are lighter to push, but cheap ones snag on flake texture. On a smooth floor either is fine; on a textured floor a cotton head usually lasts longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not treat the head. Commercial dust mops are often sprayed with an oil-based dust mop treatment that helps them hold dust on vinyl tile. On a glossy coating, that oil transfers to the floor and builds up a film that becomes slick when wet and attracts dirt. A plain, dry, clean head is what you want, washed when it stops picking up.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Passes to Dust a 22 ft Wide Two-Car Garage</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">8-9</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">36 in dust mop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">12-13</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">24 in dust mop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">12 in household sweeper</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Leaf blower, but grit is moved, not collected</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A leaf blower is a legitimate shortcut for a garage that opens onto a driveway, and many owners use one. It moves grit out rather than collecting it, so it is less effective in winter when wet salt sticks, and it pushes dust onto everything on the shelves. Think of it as a supplement to the dust mop, not a replacement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flat-mop" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Microfiber Flat Mops for Wet Cleaning
          </h2>

          <p>
            For the monthly wet clean, a microfiber flat mop is the best match for epoxy. A flat pad holds a controlled amount of solution and releases it as a thin film that dries in minutes, which is exactly what you want on a non-porous, slippery surface. Microfiber also lifts oily road film better than cotton string because its fibers are finer and physically trap residue rather than smearing it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pad size is a tradeoff. An 18 inch frame, like the Rubbermaid Adaptable kit, is a good garage size: wide enough to move quickly, small enough to wring and to get around tire chocks and cabinet legs. Wider 24 and 36 inch wet frames exist in commercial lines, but a wide wet pad is heavy and harder to keep evenly damp, and on a residential floor the time saved is small.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan on more than one pad. A garage floor is dirtier than an interior floor, and a single pad loads up with oily film partway through. Once it stops picking up and starts leaving a gray smear, switch to a clean one; continuing just spreads dirt around more evenly. Two pads is the practical minimum for a two-car garage, and three is better in salt country.
          </p>

          <p>
            How you wash the pads matters as much as the pad. Microfiber is ruined by fabric softener, which coats the fibers and makes them leave streaks, and many makers also advise against bleach. Wash them warm with a little detergent, rinse well and air dry or tumble low. Rubbermaid rates its Adaptable pads for up to 200 commercial launderings, which is a meaningful number if you are mopping monthly, because a cheap household pad can be worn out in a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid the temptation to use a sponge mop. Sponge heads hold a lot of water, leave puddles at the end of each stroke, and their abrasive scrub strips, often built into the head, are exactly the kind of pad that dulls a topcoat. A flat microfiber mop is lighter, faster and safer on the finish.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="squeegee" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Squeegees: Moss, Foam and EPDM Blades
          </h2>

          <p>
            A floor squeegee is the most underrated garage tool. After a hose rinse, a deep clean or a winter of melting snow off the car, standing water left to dry deposits minerals and salt across the floor, and that haze looks very like a failing topcoat. Pulling water off with a squeegee before it evaporates leaves the floor clear and cuts drying time from hours to minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blade material is the main choice. Moss rubber, a soft cellular rubber used on the Rubbermaid FG9C3200BLA, is the classic non-marking blade for smooth commercial floors; it conforms well and does not leave black streaks on a light coating. Closed-cell foam, used on the Libman 1014, is softer still and follows texture better, which makes it the right choice for flake and anti-slip floors, but it wears faster on rough concrete. Solid EPDM or neoprene blades, common on janitorial squeegees from brands like Unger, are stiffer and longer wearing, and they are excellent on smooth floors and less forgiving on texture.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Blade</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best floor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tradeoff</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Moss rubber (dual)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Smooth or light flake</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Handle often sold separately</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Closed-cell foam</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full flake, anti-slip texture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wears faster on rough aprons</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid EPDM / neoprene</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Smooth gloss</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stiffer; can bridge over texture</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Straight blades are better than curved ones in most garages. A straight blade pulls a clean line toward the door, which is where most garage slopes drain. Curved blades are designed to gather water toward a floor drain, which is useful in the minority of garages with a center drain. A 24 inch blade is the practical width; wider squeegees move more water but are tiring to push when the water is heavy with grit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the handle before you check out. Many commercial squeegee heads, including the Rubbermaid, are sold without one and take a standard tapered handle. Libman's 1014 includes its steel handle, which is one reason it is the cheaper complete package. Keep the blade clean and hang the squeegee off the floor so the edge does not take a set, and replace the blade when the edge rounds over, because a worn blade leaves stripes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where the garage has no slope to the door, or water collects against a back wall, a wet/dry shop vac is the other way to get water off. Our guide to the <a href="/best-small-shop-vac/">best small shop vac</a> covers compact units that are easy to keep in the garage, and a wide floor nozzle from our <a href="/best-shop-vac-attachments/">shop vac attachments</a> roundup turns one into a fast wet pickup tool.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solutions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Goes in the Bucket, According to Coating Makers
          </h2>

          <p>
            The mop is only half the decision; the other half is what you put on it. Here the most reliable guidance comes from the people who make and install coatings, and it is fairly consistent. ArmorPoxy, a manufacturer of epoxy floor systems, tells owners to avoid cleaners containing acids, including vinegar and citrus cleaners, because they leave a residue that dulls the floor, and to avoid soap-based cleaners, which can make the floor slippery when wet. It names two cleaners it considers safe: ammonia and Simple Green.
          </p>

          <p>
            The usual ammonia dilution in installer guidance is about half a cup of clear, non-sudsing ammonia per gallon of warm water, and our cleaning guide uses the same figure, but dilutions vary between sources, and your coating's own care sheet takes priority over any general advice, ours included. If your installer or kit maker gives a cleaner and ratio, use it. Many professional polyaspartic and polyurea topcoats have their own care instructions, and a warranty can depend on following them.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the tools on this page, the practical point is how the solution interacts with the mop. Microfiber works well with a dilute ammonia or a pH-neutral cleaner because neither leaves a film for the pad to smear. Concentrated cleaners and degreasers left on the floor are the opposite: they need rinsing, which means a hose and a squeegee rather than a flat mop alone. For a spray mop, mix the solution weaker rather than stronger, since the mist goes down unevenly and there is no rinse step.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never mix ammonia with bleach or with any cleaner that contains bleach, and ventilate the garage while you work. If the smell is a problem, a pH-neutral floor cleaner at label dilution does the same routine job. Detailed guidance on stains, degreasers and the annual wash is in our guide to <a href="/how-to-clean-epoxy-garage-floor/">cleaning an epoxy garage floor</a>, so we will not repeat it here.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Your coating's care sheet beats every general guide.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">ArmorPoxy's general guidance is ammonia or Simple Green, and no acids or soap. If your installer or kit maker publishes a cleaner and dilution, follow theirs; warranties can depend on it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="never" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Tools and Products to Keep Off an Epoxy Floor
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most damage to epoxy comes from cleaning tools and products, not from cars. Coating makers and installers repeat the same short list of things to avoid, and many of them are sitting in a typical garage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Acidic and citrus cleaners</h3>

          <p>
            Vinegar, citrus degreasers and acidic rust and concrete cleaners are the most common culprits. As ArmorPoxy puts it, they leave a residue that dulls the floor, and repeated use can etch a topcoat. The trap is that a citrus degreaser works well on oil, so people keep using it until the whole floor has gone flat. If the bottle says citrus, d-limonene or acid, leave it for bare concrete.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Abrasive pads, wire brushes and steel wool</h3>

          <p>
            Green scouring pads, stiff wire brushes and steel wool all cut a clear topcoat and leave a dull patch more visible than the stain. Steel wool also leaves fragments that rust into orange specks. For scrubbing, use a nylon deck brush. Melamine sponges are mildly abrasive too; use them gently and only on a small spot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Soap, oil soap and mop treatments</h3>

          <p>
            Dish soap and oil soap leave films that build up into a slick, cloudy layer, and oil-based dust mop treatments do the same from the dry side. On a glossy floor, a film is a slip hazard before it is a cosmetic problem. If your floor has gone hazy and slick after months of soapy mopping, the fix is a rinse with clean water or diluted ammonia, not a stronger soap.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rental floor machines with aggressive pads</h3>

          <p>
            Walk-behind scrubbers are fine on epoxy with a soft pad and neutral solution, but rental machines often come with black or brown stripping pads designed to remove floor finish. On a coating they remove gloss. If you rent one, ask for a white or red pad and confirm before you start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Steam mops are a judgment call. Some coating makers say nothing about them; others caution against prolonged heat on topcoats. Since a steam mop adds nothing a microfiber pad and dilute ammonia cannot do on a garage floor, we would leave it in the house.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="textured" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Flake, Anti-Slip and Bare-Edge Floors
          </h2>

          <p>
            A smooth, full-gloss floor is easy: dust mop, flat mop, straight moss squeegee. Texture changes the tools. Full-broadcast flake floors have a slightly bumpy surface after the topcoat, and floors with an anti-slip additive in the clear coat feel like fine sandpaper. Both are safer underfoot, and both are harder on mops.
          </p>

          <p>
            On textured floors, cheap microfiber is the first casualty. Thin pads snag and pill on aggregate, and the loose fibers end up stuck in the texture. Look for denser commercial pads with a strong hook-and-loop backing, like the Rubbermaid pads, and expect them to wear faster than on a smooth floor. A cotton dust mop usually holds up better than a microfiber dust pad on heavy texture.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the wet clean on a heavily textured floor, a flat mop alone may not lift dirt out of the low spots. A soft nylon deck brush on a pole, worked with solution and then rinsed and squeegeed with a foam blade like the Libman 1014, is often more effective. It is more work, but it is the only way to get grime out of texture without abrasives.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the edges. Many garage coatings stop short of the walls or the door threshold, leaving bare concrete at the perimeter and on the apron. Bare concrete is abrasive and porous, and it shreds pads and foam blades quickly. Stop the mop and squeegee at the coating edge, and use a broom on the concrete beyond it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are still choosing a coating and know you hate mopping, texture is worth weighing in that decision. Light flake hides dust between cleanings, and an anti-slip additive makes a wet floor far safer, but both make the floor a little harder to clean. Our page on <a href="/garage-floor-epoxy-colors/">garage floor epoxy colors</a> covers how color and flake density affect how dirty a floor looks between cleanings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spin-vs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Spin Mops, String Mops and Robots in the Garage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Spin mops are the most common mop people already own, so it is worth being clear about how they fit. A spin mop is a good tool for a kitchen, and our guide to the <a href="/best-spin-mop/">best spin mops</a> covers the best of them. In a garage they have two weaknesses: the round heads are small, typically around a foot across, and the single-bucket designs recycle dirty water, which on a garage floor full of salt and road film means you spread a thin layer of grime across the whole slab. They work for a single-car garage with a light routine; for anything bigger, a flat mop is faster and cleaner.
          </p>

          <p>
            String mops have a place in garages that see heavy water, such as snowmelt or a hose rinse with no squeegee. A cotton string mop soaks up a lot of water quickly. The problems are lint, which shows up on dark coatings, and the tendency to leave a wet trail that dries with streaks. If you use one, wring it hard and follow with a squeegee or a dry flat pad.
          </p>

          <p>
            Robot vacuums and robot mops are increasingly common, and a few owners run them in garages. They can handle light dust on a smooth, clear floor, but garages are full of things robots struggle with: thresholds, cords, tools on the floor, temperature swings and coarse grit that wears their brushes. We would not count on one as the main dust tool in a garage, and robot mop pads are too small and too lightly loaded to clean road film.
          </p>

          <p>
            The one place a household tool does make sense is the spray mop. A light spray mop hanging by the door handles spot jobs between real cleanings, which is why the O-Cedar ProMist MAX is on our list. Its job is not the whole floor; it is the drip, the footprint and the tire track you would otherwise leave until the weekend.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. The Full Kit and What It Costs to Keep Running
          </h2>

          <p>
            Put together, a complete epoxy floor kit is short: a wide dust mop for weekly grit removal, a microfiber flat mop with at least two pads for the monthly wet clean, a squeegee matched to your floor's texture, and optionally a spray mop for spot cleaning. Add a nylon deck brush on a pole for the annual deep wash and a shop vac for corners and wet pickup, and you have everything the routine needs.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tool</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Job</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">How often</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">36 in dust mop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove grit and salt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Weekly, more in winter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">18 in microfiber flat mop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wet clean with neutral solution</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Monthly</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">24 in squeegee</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pull rinse water and snowmelt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">After rinses, through winter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spray mop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drips, footprints, tire tracks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">As needed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nylon deck brush</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Textured floors, deep clean</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Annually or as needed</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Running costs are mostly heads and pads. A cotton dust mop head that is shaken out after every use and washed occasionally lasts a long time; replace it when it stops picking up or starts to come apart. Microfiber pads should be replaced when they feel flat and stop absorbing, which with commercial pads can be years of monthly mopping. Squeegee blades get replaced when the edge rounds over and starts to streak.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storage matters more than most people think. Hang the dust mop, flat mop and squeegee off the floor so the heads dry and the blades do not take a set. A wet mop head left on the floor grows mildew and leaves a musty patch, and a squeegee stored blade-down develops a bend that leaves stripes. A simple wall rack near the door also keeps the tools in sight, which makes it more likely the weekly dust mop actually happens.
          </p>

          <p>
            The goal of all this is to protect a coating that cost real money. A floor that is dust mopped weekly and wet mopped with the right solution keeps its gloss far longer, and if the topcoat eventually wears, a clear-coat refresh is cheap compared with a full recoat. Our guides to <a href="/how-long-does-a-garage-epoxy-floor-last/">how long a garage epoxy floor lasts</a> and <a href="/how-to-seal-epoxy-garage-floor/">sealing an epoxy garage floor</a> cover that next step.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Wet mopping before dust mopping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A wet mop over grit turns sand and salt into a slurry and drags it across the topcoat. Always dust mop or blow the floor out first, then wet mop. The dry pass is what protects the gloss; the wet pass only removes film.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a citrus degreaser for the monthly mop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Citrus cleaners cut oil well, so they feel like the right choice, but coating makers such as ArmorPoxy say acidic and citrus cleaners leave a residue that dulls the floor. Keep them for bare concrete and mop epoxy with dilute ammonia, Simple Green or a pH-neutral cleaner.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Washing microfiber with fabric softener</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Softener coats microfiber and leaves it smearing instead of absorbing, which shows as streaks on a gloss floor. Wash pads warm with a little detergent, no softener, and air dry or tumble low. One softener wash can ruin a set of pads.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the dust mop with oil</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dust mop treatment sprays are made for vinyl tile. On epoxy, the oil transfers and builds up a film that turns the floor slick when wet. Use a plain, clean, dry head and wash it when it stops picking up grit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting rinse water air dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water left on the floor evaporates and leaves minerals and salt behind as a haze that looks like a worn topcoat. Squeegee rinse water and snowmelt toward the door, or pick it up with a shop vac, so the floor dries clear.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mop for an epoxy garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A wide dust mop for weekly grit removal and a microfiber flat mop for wet cleaning. Our picks are the O-Cedar Commercial MaxiDust 36 inch kit for dusting and the Rubbermaid Adaptable 18 inch flat mop kit for wet mopping, with a 24 inch squeegee for rinse water. Together they cover the whole routine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a spin mop on an epoxy floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, it will not damage the coating, but it is a poor fit for a garage. The heads are small, so a two-car floor takes a long time, and single-bucket designs reuse dirty water, which spreads road film. A microfiber flat mop is faster and cleaner on a big garage floor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is microfiber safe on epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Microfiber is non-abrasive and lifts oily film well, which makes it the preferred wet mop material on coatings. The caution is texture: cheap pads snag and pill on heavy flake or anti-slip floors. Use dense commercial pads there, and wash them without fabric softener so they keep absorbing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I mop an epoxy garage floor with?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Coating makers generally recommend dilute ammonia or a mild cleaner; ArmorPoxy names ammonia and Simple Green and says to avoid acidic, citrus and soap-based cleaners. Follow your own coating's care sheet if it gives a cleaner and dilution, because that takes priority over general advice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a squeegee for an epoxy floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you ever hose the floor or get snowmelt off a car, yes. Water left to evaporate leaves a mineral and salt haze. A 24 inch squeegee pulls it to the door in a minute. Use moss rubber on smooth floors and a softer foam blade on flake or anti-slip floors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How wide should a garage dust mop be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For an open two- or three-car garage, 36 inches is the practical width: about eight or nine passes across a 22 foot floor. A 24 inch head is easier in a crowded single bay. Wider than 36 inches gets awkward to turn and store in a residential garage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a steam mop on epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We would not. A steam mop adds nothing a microfiber pad and dilute ammonia cannot do on a garage floor, and some coating installers caution against prolonged heat on topcoats. If your coating maker approves it, follow their guidance; otherwise keep the steam mop indoors.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

